        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A self-leveling concrete crack filler is a pourable, flexible sealant that flows into a crack and settles into a flat, smooth surface without tooling. On driveways, patios, sidewalks and garage aprons it is often the best repair there is, because it moves with the slab instead of fighting it. It also fails in predictable ways: on walls, on slopes, in cracks that are too deep or too narrow, and in cold weather. This guide covers all of them.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Self-Leveling Crack Sealant Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Self-leveling crack sealants are elastomeric joint sealants made thin enough to flow. Most are either polyurethane or a hybrid polymer, sometimes called silyl-modified or advanced polymer chemistry. They come in caulk tubes or sausage packs, go in with a caulk gun, and cure by reacting with moisture in the air. Once cured they stay rubbery, which is the entire point: they stretch when a crack opens in cold weather and compress when it closes in summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            They are not the same thing as self-leveling underlayment, the cement-based pour used to flatten whole floors before tile or vinyl. They are also different from thin liquid crack seals, which are usually acrylic or latex products in squeeze bottles meant for hairlines, and from rigid epoxy fillers, which bond powerfully but cannot flex. Self-leveling sealants occupy the middle: thick enough to bridge real gaps, thin enough to settle flat, and flexible enough to survive movement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Self-leveling sealants matter most on moving cracks. Outdoor slabs expand and contract daily and seasonally, and a crack in a driveway can open and close by a noticeable fraction of its width between January and July. A rigid filler in that crack either cracks itself or tears off one side. A self-leveling sealant designed for joint movement simply stretches. Sika rates its Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant for movement of up to 25 percent of the average joint width, which is typical of quality products in this class.
          </p>

          <p>
            The self-leveling part is what makes it practical on flat surfaces. A gunnable non-sag caulk has to be tooled smooth, and on a long crack that leaves a ridge or a dip that catches shovels and collects dirt. A self-leveling product flows to the edges of the crack under its own weight and cures into a flat surface just below the concrete. The result sheds water, resists snow shovels, and looks tidy without any skill at the tooling step. The trade-off, covered next, is that it only works where gravity is on your side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="horizontal-only" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Horizontal Only, and the Slope Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            A self-leveling sealant goes wherever gravity sends it. On a vertical surface, such as a foundation wall or the face of a step, it runs out of the crack before it skins over. That is not a defect; it is how the product works. For walls, steps and the vertical edge where a slab meets a foundation, you need a non-sag polyurethane or hybrid sealant that is designed to stay put and be tooled. Our <a href="/best-caulk-for-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best caulk for concrete</a> guide covers both kinds side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Slopes are where people get caught out. Many driveways, garage aprons and sidewalks have a slight pitch for drainage, often a few percent, and a long crack running downhill on that pitch is a channel. Pour a self-leveling sealant into it and the material creeps toward the low end before it skins, leaving the top of the crack underfilled and the bottom overfilled or spilling out. Owners commonly report this as the main frustration with self-leveling products, and it is the reason labels limit them to horizontal or near-level work.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are workable fixes. Fill a sloped crack in short sections, a foot or two at a time, and let each section skin before continuing uphill. Start at the low end so each new section is held back by material that has already begun to set. Use backer rod to keep the sealant shallow, because a thin bead skins faster and flows less. On genuinely steep stretches, switch to a non-sag sealant for that part of the crack and tool it flush. Mixing the two on one crack is fine as long as both are compatible polyurethanes or hybrids.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cracks that run across the slope, perpendicular to the fall, behave much better. The sealant has nowhere to flow except along a level line, so it settles evenly. If you are deciding which cracks to tackle with a self-leveling product and which with a non-sag one, a quick look with a level or even a marble along each crack settles it. Flat and cross-slope cracks get self-leveling sealant; steep downhill cracks get non-sag or get filled in short stages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="width-and-depth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Width, Depth and Why Backer Rod Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Self-leveling sealants need a crack wide enough to accept them. Below about 1/8 inch, the material struggles to flow into the gap and mostly sits on top, bonded to the surface rather than the crack faces. Hairline cracks are better served by a thin liquid crack seal or by routing the crack wider first. At the other end, very wide gaps of more than about an inch can exceed what a given product is rated for, and the label will list a maximum joint width. Within that range, these sealants are at their best.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth matters as much as width. Joint sealants work best in a shape that is wider than it is deep, and manufacturers specify the ratio. Sika calls for a two-to-one width-to-depth ratio, with a sealant depth between 1/4 and 1/2 inch in moving joints and the full 1/2 inch in horizontal joints that carry traffic. A thin, wide bead stretches easily; a deep, narrow plug of sealant cannot, because it is too stiff along its depth and tears away from the sides as the crack opens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backer rod is how you set that depth. It is a round, compressible foam rope, usually closed-cell polyethylene, sized about 25 percent wider than the crack so it stays put when pushed in. Press it down to the depth you want with a blunt tool, such as a putty knife or a roller, then pour the sealant on top. Backer rod also does a second job: the sealant does not stick to it, so the bead bonds only to the two crack faces. That two-sided adhesion is what lets the sealant stretch rather than tear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Irregular cracks that are too narrow at the bottom for backer rod can be packed with clean, dry sand to the right depth instead. It is less tidy but sets the depth and saves sealant. Quikrete rates a 10.1-ounce tube of its Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling Sealant at about 12 linear feet in a joint 1/2 inch wide and 1/4 inch deep, which shows how quickly an untreated deep crack eats product. Fill a 1-inch-deep crack without backer rod and that tube covers a few feet instead of a dozen.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2:1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Width-to-depth ratio Sika specifies</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/4-1/2 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical sealant depth over backer rod</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Backer rod oversize versus crack width</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/8 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rough minimum crack width for flow</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature-and-cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Temperature, Moisture and Cure Times
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most self-leveling sealants list an application range of roughly 40 to 100 degrees F, measured at the concrete surface. The surface temperature matters more than the air. A slab in shade on a cool autumn morning can be well below the forecast high, and a driveway in afternoon sun can be far hotter than the air. An infrared thermometer is worth having. Cold slabs slow the cure and thicken the sealant so it flows poorly; very hot slabs speed skinning so much the material may not level fully.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best timing for outdoor cracks is a mild, dry day in spring or fall, with the application done in the morning or late afternoon. Cracks are at a middle width in mild weather, so the sealant goes in at an average position and does not have to stretch or compress to an extreme in its first season. Filling a crack on the hottest day of summer, when it is at its narrowest, means the sealant spends all winter stretched wide. Filling on the coldest day means it gets squeezed up out of the crack in summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture-cure sealants need some humidity to cure but cannot go into a wet crack. Standing water, damp crack faces and dew all interfere with adhesion. After rain or pressure washing, give the concrete at least a full day of drying weather, longer in cool conditions, and make sure there is no rain forecast for several hours after application. A damp crack is the most common reason a bead peels free along one edge weeks later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cure happens in stages. The surface skins or becomes tack-free first; Quikrete says its Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling Sealant reaches tack-free in about an hour, which is quick for this category. Polyurethane sealants commonly take longer to skin and several days to cure through, longer in cool or dry weather. Keep foot traffic off until the surface is firm and vehicles off for as long as the label specifies, which is often several days. A tire turning on a soft bead can drag it out of the crack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="products" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Two Proven Self-Leveling Crack Sealants
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two widely sold products cover most homeowner needs, and both are reviewed in more detail in our <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete crack filler</a> rankings. Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant is a single-component polyurethane joint sealant with a published movement rating of up to 25 percent of joint width. It flows readily and settles flat, and Sika is unusually specific about installation depth and ratio. Its main weakness is shared by every product in this class: on a sloped driveway it creeps downhill before skinning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quikrete Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling Sealant does the same job with a hybrid polymer chemistry. It is solvent and isocyanate free, which makes it more pleasant to use in an attached garage or a basement floor where fumes linger. Quikrete lists a tack-free time of about an hour, which shortens the window in which leaves, grit and insects end up stuck in the bead. Like the Sikaflex, it is for horizontal surfaces only and benefits from backer rod to control consumption.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chemistry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stand-out trait</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sikaflex Self-Leveling Sealant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rated for movement up to 25 percent of joint width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Driveways, sidewalks, joints with seasonal movement</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Quikrete Advanced Polymer Self-Leveling Sealant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hybrid polymer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tack-free in about an hour; solvent and isocyanate free</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Garage and basement floors, quick outdoor jobs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thin liquid crack seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually acrylic or latex</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrates hairlines</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cracks under about 1/8 inch</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Non-sag polyurethane caulk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane or hybrid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stays in vertical joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Walls, steps, steep sections</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Either is a sound choice, and the decision is mostly about where you are working. For long outdoor joints with a lot of seasonal movement, the Sikaflex rating and its long track record make it the default. For enclosed spaces and jobs where a fast skin matters, the Quikrete is easier to live with. Both come in standard caulk tubes and larger sizes; a long control joint or several long cracks justifies the larger pack and a caulk gun to match.
          </p>

          <p>
            Color is the other consideration. Both brands sell gray or limestone-type colors meant to blend with concrete, but no sealant matches weathered concrete exactly, and a fresh bead will look darker or lighter than the slab for a while. If appearance matters, try a short test section in an inconspicuous crack first. Avoid painting over flexible sealants unless the label says it is allowed, since many paints and coatings crack or fail to bond on an elastomeric surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Apply Self-Leveling Crack Filler
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with cleaning, because adhesion is everything. Remove weeds, loose concrete and old failed filler with a wire brush, a screwdriver or a crack-cleaning tool. For a crack with crumbly edges, a grinder with a diamond blade or a crack chaser can open it to clean, sound faces. Blow or vacuum the crack out completely. If you pressure wash, let the slab dry thoroughly before going further; trapped moisture under the bead is a common cause of failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, set the depth. Measure the crack width and choose backer rod about 25 percent larger. Push it in evenly with a blunt tool so the top sits at the right depth below the surface, usually 1/4 to 1/2 inch for most crack widths, keeping the two-to-one width-to-depth ratio in mind. In narrow or irregular cracks, pack clean sand to the same depth instead. Do not stretch the backer rod as you install it, since it shrinks back later and can leave voids under the sealant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle small, at an angle, and start filling from the low end of the crack. Hold the gun steady and let the sealant flow into the crack rather than trying to spread it. Stop just short of the surface, about 1/8 inch below, because the material keeps settling for a few minutes and an overfilled crack leaves a raised strip that wears quickly under tires and shovels. On sloped cracks, work in short sections as described above. No tooling is needed on level cracks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the fresh bead. Keep people, pets and vehicles off it for the label times, and watch for rain. If a section dips more than you expected after the first pour, a second pass once the first has skinned is fine with most products. The same approach applies whether you are working a garage apron or the cracks covered in our <a href="/sealing-cracks-in-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">driveway crack sealing tutorial</a>. For a garage floor you intend to coat, use a coatable crack filler instead, as covered in <a href="/garage-floor-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor crack repair</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Clean, dry, backer rod, fill from the low end, stop 1/8 inch shy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Most failures of self-leveling crack sealant come from skipping one of those five steps, not from the product. A clean, dry crack with the right depth set by backer rod and a slightly underfilled bead is a repair that commonly lasts years.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="when-not-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When to Use Something Else
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hairlines are the first case. A crack too narrow to accept the sealant will end up with a smear on top that peels off. Use a thin liquid crack seal, or route the crack to at least 1/4 inch wide if you want a flexible sealant in it. Vertical cracks are the second case, covered above: use non-sag. The third is a leaking basement wall or floor crack with water actively coming through, where hydraulic cement or an injection system is the right tool; our <a href="/basement-crack-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack sealer guide</a> covers those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Structural cracks are the fourth. A self-leveling sealant keeps water and debris out of a crack, but it does not hold anything together. If one side of a crack is dropping, if the crack keeps widening, or if a slab is heaving or sinking, sealing the crack treats the symptom. Address the cause first, whether that is drainage, voids under the slab or frost heave. Our overview of <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair</a> explains how to tell a stable crack from an active one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Floors you plan to coat are the fifth. Most self-leveling sealants are designed to stay exposed, and many epoxies and paints will not bond reliably to them. Under a garage floor coating, use a rigid epoxy or polyurea crack filler for dormant cracks and keep flexible material in the control joints only. Asphalt is the sixth. Concrete sealants are not designed for asphalt driveways, which have their own rubberized fillers; see our <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best asphalt crack filler</a> picks for those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be skeptical of products that promise to do everything. Viral fillers sold through social ads often claim to fix any crack in any surface, which no single product can do. If you are weighing one of those, our <a href="/magic-crack-filler-reviews/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">Magic Crack Filler review</a> looks at what buyers report. For flat, moving outdoor cracks between about 1/8 inch and an inch wide, a proper self-leveling polyurethane or hybrid sealant over backer rod remains the repair to beat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using it on a wall or steep slope</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Self-leveling sealant flows wherever gravity takes it. On a vertical crack it runs straight out, and on a sloped driveway crack it creeps to the low end before it skins. Use a non-sag sealant for walls and steps, and fill steep downhill cracks in short sections starting from the bottom.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling deep cracks without backer rod</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pouring sealant into an untreated deep crack wastes tubes and produces a deep, stiff plug that tears free when the crack opens. Backer rod sets the right depth, keeps the sealant bonded to only two sides, and makes a single tube cover many times more length. It costs very little per foot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying to a damp crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moisture in the crack faces is the most common reason a bead peels free along one edge. After rain or pressure washing, give the slab at least a full day of dry weather, longer in cool temperatures. If the concrete looks darker in the crack than around it, it is still too wet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overfilling to the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Self-leveling sealant keeps settling for several minutes after you pour it, and a bead filled flush often ends up proud of the surface. Raised sealant is scuffed by tires and caught by shovels. Stop about 1/8 inch below the surface and let it level, adding a second pass later if needed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing in extreme temperatures</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Below about 40 degrees F the sealant flows poorly and cures slowly; on a scorching slab it skins before it levels. Cracks filled at their narrowest in summer heat are stretched hard all winter. Aim for a mild, dry day and check the slab temperature with an infrared thermometer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is self-leveling concrete crack filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a flexible joint sealant, usually polyurethane or a hybrid polymer, formulated thin enough to flow into a horizontal crack and settle into a flat surface without tooling. It cures by reacting with moisture in the air and stays rubbery, so it can stretch and compress as the slab moves with temperature changes through the seasons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use self-leveling sealant on a vertical crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. It will run out of the crack before it cures. For foundation walls, step faces and the joint where a slab meets a wall, use a non-sag polyurethane or hybrid sealant that is designed to stay in place and be tooled smooth. Many brands sell both versions in the same color, which helps when a crack turns a corner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How wide a crack can self-leveling sealant fill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 1/8 inch up to about an inch for many products, but check the label for its stated maximum joint width. Narrower cracks will not accept the sealant and need a thin liquid crack seal or routing. Wider gaps need backer rod at minimum and may need a different repair, such as a patching compound.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I really need backer rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cracks deeper than about 1/2 inch, yes. Backer rod sets the sealant depth to the two-to-one width-to-depth shape manufacturers specify, prevents three-sided adhesion so the bead can stretch, and dramatically reduces how much sealant each foot of crack uses. In narrow irregular cracks, clean dry sand can do the same job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can drive on it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product and the weather. Some hybrid sealants become tack-free in about an hour, but full cure through the bead often takes days, longer in cool or dry conditions. Follow the label for vehicle traffic, and when in doubt wait longer. A turning tire on a soft bead can pull it out of the crack.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will self-leveling sealant work on a sloped driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It works, but it needs care. On a crack running downhill it will flow toward the low end before skinning. Fill in short sections starting at the bottom, keep the bead shallow with backer rod, and let each section skin before continuing. On the steepest stretches, switch to a non-sag sealant and tool it flush.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint or epoxy over self-leveling crack sealant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not, unless the label specifically says it can be coated. Most flexible sealants are meant to remain exposed, and rigid coatings either fail to bond or crack as the sealant moves. If you plan to coat a garage floor, use a rigid epoxy or polyurea crack filler for dormant cracks and reserve flexible sealant for joints.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

